\section{Related Work}
\subsection{Egocentric Datasets and Benchmarks}
%Egocentric vision has evolved from clip-level action recognition~\cite{Damen2020RESCALING} to massive unscripted environments~\cite{grauman2022ego4d, grauman2024egoexo4d, engel2023projectaria, yang2025egolife}. To evaluate assistant-style interactions, human-annotated VideoQA benchmarks like QAEGO4D~\cite{Baermann_2022_CVPR}, HoloAssist~\cite{kwon2023holoassist}, and EgoSchema~\cite{mangalam2024egoschema} have been proposed. However, they are restricted to minute-scale clips, leaving long-form episodic memory underexplored. In addition, recent datasets trying to  generate egocentric QA often rely on LLM/VLM ~\cite{ye2025mmego, li2026egocross, peng2025egogazevqa, rodin2025easgbench, wang2026egomemreason},  introducing circular biases. Furthermore, both paradigms lack the tools for fine-grained error analysis. CapMem solves these limitations: our human-annotated, up-to-hour-long testbed eliminates language and knowledge bias, and our timestamps and attribute tags enable precise error diagnosis.

Egocentric vision has progressed from clip-level action recognition~\cite{Damen2020RESCALING} to large-scale datasets~\cite{grauman2022ego4d, grauman2024egoexo4d, engel2023projectaria, yang2025egolife}. Existing assistant-oriented datasets and QA benchmarks, including QAEGO4D~\cite{Baermann_2022_CVPR}, HoloAssist~\cite{kwon2023holoassist}, and EgoSchema~\cite{mangalam2024egoschema}, mainly focus on short or minute-scale video contexts. More recent egocentric QA efforts, such as MM-Ego~\cite{ye2025mmego} and EgoGazeVQA~\cite{peng2025eye}, scale annotation with automatic or MLLM-generated QA pairs, but may inherit generation-induced language bias. In contrast, CapMem provides a human-annotated, long-form video benchmark.



%Egocentric vision has advanced rapidly with large-scale datasets and standardized benchmarks. EPIC-KITCHENS-100~\cite{Damen2020RESCALING} established widely used evaluations for fine-grained action recognition and anticipation. Ego4D~\cite{grauman2022ego4d} expanded the field to over 3{,}000 hours of unscripted first-person video and introduced a broad suite of tasks, including episodic-memory querying that localizes relevant evidence within a user’s recorded past. Ego-Exo4D~\cite{grauman2024egoexo4d} further adds synchronized egocentric--exocentric views for studying skilled activities from complementary perspectives.

%Several benchmarks push beyond recognition toward assistant-style interaction and question answering. HoloAssist~\cite{kwon2023holoassist} captures real-time instructor--performer collaboration with conversational and action annotations. QAEGO4D~\cite{Baermann_2022_CVPR} proposes episodic-memory-based VideoQA on long egocentric videos, leveraging temporal-window annotations to ground answers in supporting evidence. EgoSchema~\cite{mangalam2024egoschema} provides human-curated multiple-choice QA grounded in three-minute egocentric clips, serving as a diagnostic for minute-scale long-context temporal reasoning. Complementary efforts such as Project Aria~\cite{engel2023projectaria} and EgoLife~\cite{yang2025egolife} broaden wearable sensing and long-term assistant-oriented settings. Despite this progress, most evaluations remain clip- or minutes-centric, leaving systematic assessment of hour-scale reasoning and QA under realistic token budgets underexplored.




\subsection{Episodic Memory Tasks}
Episodic memory is central to  wearable assistants~\cite{engel2023projectaria, grauman2022ego4d}. Ego4D established this paradigm with memory-oriented tasks such as NLQ, VQ, and MQ~\cite{grauman2022ego4d}. Subsequent work extends this line to egocentric episodic-memory QA~\cite{Baermann_2022_CVPR}, narration-supervised query localization~\cite{ramakrishnan2023naq}, and cross-view memory reasoning~\cite{grauman2024egoexo4d, egoexomem2026}. While these efforts mainly focus on visual retrieval or direct VideoQA, we evaluate whether caption-based memory can support long-form egocentric QA (Appendix \ref{appendixA}).



\subsection{Video Caption and Harness Engineering}
Recent work tackles the memory and token limits of long-horizon videos by abstracting visual content into textual representations. SiLVR \cite{silvr2025} and MR. Video \cite{pang2025mrvideo} use dense clip-level captions for long-context reasoning with LLMs, while LLMVS \cite{llmvs2025} applies frame-level captions to LLM-based video summarization. However, the effectiveness of caption-based reasoning in continuous, long-form egocentric settings remains underexplored. Our task and benchmark fill this gap, providing a dedicated testbed
for, to our knowledge, the first systematic study of how harness
design can enhance caption-centric reasoning.




